    "https://usccareers.usc.edu/job/los-angeles/part-time-lecturer-structural-design-and-engineering-design-and-build/1209/95641856464": {
      "title": "Part-Time Lecturer, Structural Design and Engineering",
      "description": "Part-Time Lecturer, Structural Design and Engineering – Design & Build at USC Skip to main content Life at USC Career Areas Faculty Keck Medicine of USC Auxiliary Services Information Technology Services Human Resources, Ethics, and Compliance USC Advancement Current Employees Returning Applicants Saved Jobs 0 Job Alerts Join Our Trojan Community At USC, we are united by shared values and a bold vision for the future. Join a workplace where your role has meaning, your growth is supported, and your impact is felt across campus, Los Angeles, and the world. Search by Keyword Search Jobs Faculty Educators, mentors, and agents of change. As USC faculty, we are committed to advancing knowledge, inspiring students, and elevating research that fosters discovery and drives societal impact – all in service of our mission. Learn more on the We Are USC Faculty webpage Search Jobs Search by Keyword Location Radius Select Radius 5 miles 15 miles 25 miles 35 miles 50 miles Search Jobs Part-Time Lecturer, Structural Design and Engineering – Design & Build Los Angeles, CA Apply Save Job Current employees apply here Job ID REQ20175649 Category Faculty Helpful Information for Applicants External Applicants Internal Applicants (Current USC Employees) The USC School of Architecture invites applicants for a part-time faculty position in Structural Design and Engineering to lead a unique joint Civil Engineering and Building Science Design Studio for Spring 2027. Candidates must demonstrate the ability to teach an upper-level undergraduate capstone design studio integrating architectural, structural, and civil engineering design. The course will include evaluation, design, and analysis of building loads, including gravity, wind, and seismic load. Candidates must demonstrate an understanding of the state of the art of structural engineering as well as current code requirements, building systems, controls, building standards, and sustainability performance criteria. Candidates must demonstrate the ability to apply appropriate quantitative analysis techniques, (including appropriate software-based analysis tools) in the context of the architectural and engineering design process. Experience with contemporary digital design software tools is preferred. Some experience in adaptation and reusability, including heritage conservation is preferred. The core salary range for this position is $50.75 - $69.79 per hour. This is a part-time, 35% position. When extending an offer of employment, the University of Southern California considers factors such as (but not limited to) the scope and responsibilities of the position, the candidate’s work experience, education/training, key skills, internal peer equity, federal, state, and local laws, as well as external market and organizational considerations. Qualifications: A Master of Science degree, or equivalent, and a S.E. and/or P.E. license is required. Preference is given to candidates with a proven track record of professional work. Applicants will be asked to submit a CV, cover letter, 3-5 references with contact information, and selected work samples (portfolio/publications; 10 MB max). USC is an equal-opportunity educator and employer, proudly pluralistic and firmly committed to providing equal opportunity for outstanding persons of every race, gender, creed and background. The university particularly encourages members of underrepresented groups, veterans and individuals with disabilities to apply. USC will make reasonable accommodations for qualified individuals with known disabilities unless doing so would result in an undue hardship. Further information is available by contacting uschr@usc.edu . If you have questions about the position, please contact Bhavna Sharma, PhD: bhavnas@usc.edu . Job ID REQ20175649 Posted Date 05/27/2026 Apply Save Job Current employees apply here SHARE Facebook X LinkedIn Email Related Content Learn More August 04, 2025 USC Today Explore the latest stories o