    "https://usccareers.usc.edu/job/washington-d-c/part-time-teaching-track-faculty-position-in-economics-capital-campus/1209/95301606528": {
      "title": "Part-Time, Teaching-Track Faculty Position in Economics",
      "description": "Part-Time, Teaching-Track Faculty Position in Economics - Capital Campus at USC Skip to main content Life at USC Career Areas Faculty Keck Medicine of USC Auxiliary Services Information Technology Services Human Resources, Ethics, and Compliance USC Advancement Current Employees Returning Applicants Saved Jobs 0 Job Alerts Join Our Trojan Community At USC, we are united by shared values and a bold vision for the future. Join a workplace where your role has meaning, your growth is supported, and your impact is felt across campus, Los Angeles, and the world. Search by Keyword Search Jobs Faculty Educators, mentors, and agents of change. As USC faculty, we are committed to advancing knowledge, inspiring students, and elevating research that fosters discovery and drives societal impact – all in service of our mission. Learn more on the We Are USC Faculty webpage Search Jobs Search by Keyword Location Radius Select Radius 5 miles 15 miles 25 miles 35 miles 50 miles Search Jobs Part-Time, Teaching-Track Faculty Position in Economics - Capital Campus Washington D.C., DC Apply Save Job Current employees apply here Job ID REQ20175465 Category Faculty Helpful Information for Applicants External Applicants Internal Applicants (Current USC Employees) The Department of Economics in the Dana and David Dornsife College of Letters, Arts and Sciences at the University of Southern California seeks qualified applicants for part-time, one-semester, in-person Lecturer positions to join our faculty at the Washington, DC, Capital Campus in Spring 2027 for the following courses: Intermediate Macroeconomics (ECON 305) : The determinants of aggregate income, employment, and inflation; economic fluctuations; fiscal and monetary policy; financial markets; the national debt. Money, Credit and Banking (ECON 357) : The money, bond, stock, and other financial markets; portfolio choice; determinants of asset prices and interest rates; inflation; and interactions between financial markets and government policies. One section of each course will be offered, and applicants may indicate their availability to teach either or both courses. Instructors will develop syllabi and assignments in accordance with program guidelines. The class meets two days per week in two 1-hour and 20- minutes blocks at the Capital Campus, 1771 N St NW, Washington, DC. In addition, instructors are expected to hold a two hours of office hours per week to answer students’ questions on an individual basis. The successful candidate will work closely with USC Dornsife faculty in planning and delivering the course. In order to be considered for this position, all candidates must apply via the \"Apply\" link at the top or bottom of this page.  The following application materials are required: Cover Letter; CV; statement of teaching philosophy; a sample syllabus; and the names of three individuals whom USC will contact for letters of reference. Referees should speak to the applicant’s teaching abilities. For additional information, please contact: Ladan Masoudie Masoudie@usc.edu Ideal candidates will demonstrate pedagogical innovation, adaptability, and a strong awareness of varied learning needs. We value educators who foster critical thinking and are committed to creating engaging, student-centered classroom environments that support holistic student development. Initial appointments are fixed-term, with the possibility of renewal contingent on performance and review by the program faculty, its directors, and the college deans. QUALIFICATIONS Candidates should have a PhD in Economics by August 2026. We require demonstrated excellence in teaching Economics at the college level. APPLICATION PROCEDURE Applications are requested immediately. Applicants should submit a cover letter, CV, statement of teaching philosophy, a sample syllabus, and the names of three referees who can speak to their teaching abilities. The referees will be contacted directly by USC to provide letters of reference after submission of application. We expect to conduct interviews with selected candidates later this year, additional information will be provided at that time. The per-course rate for this position is $14,000. When extending an offer of employment, the University of Southern California considers factors such as (but not limited to) the scope and responsibilities of the position, the candidate’s work experience, education/training, key skills, internal peer equity, federal, state, and local laws, contractual stipulations, grant funding, as well as external market and organizational considerations. Job ID REQ20175465 Posted Date 05/19/2026 Apply Save Job Current employees apply here SHARE Facebook X LinkedIn Email Related Content Learn More August 04, 2025 USC Today Explore the latest stories of impact, innovation, and Trojan spirit shaping USC and the world beyond. Learn More July 28, 2025 USC News Blog - Hub Page Related Content Our Communities Rooted in LA, USC partners with local communities to uplift education, well-being, and opportunity across Southern California. Learn More July 28, 2025 Culture & Community Blog - Hub Page Related Content Mission Statement USC’s central mission is the development of human beings and society as a whole through the cultivation and enrichment of the human mind and spirit. Learn More July 28, 2025 Mission & Values Blog - Hub Page Related Content Alumni USC Today’s Alumni page highlights news, achievements, research and stories from USC alumni and the Trojan Family. Learn More August 03, 2026 Culture & Community Article Blog - Hub Page Related Content USC Football Stay connected with USC Football through the latest news, schedules, scores, highlights, and team updates. Learn More September 10, 2026 Culture & Community Blog - Hub Page Related Content AI at USC At USC, artificial intelligence is more than just code. It is a catalyst for discovery, creativity and social good. Learn More February 24, 2026 USC News Artic